<ArticleLayout
  title="Heating an Open-Plan Living Area: What to Check Before Choosing a System"
  seoTitle="Heating an Open-Plan Living Area | Australian Guide"
  description="Compare heating options for open-plan living. Check connected volume, ceiling height, glazing, airflow, zoning, insulation and system sizing before buying."
  category="Living Spaces"
  date="Published July 2026"
  datePublished="2026-07-29"
  readingTime="23 minute read"
  showAffiliateDisclosure={false}
  image="/images/heating-an-open-plan-living-area.webp"
  imageAlt="An open-plan Australian kitchen, dining and living area heated by a wall-mounted reverse-cycle air conditioner"
  socialImage="/images/heating-an-open-plan-living-area.webp"
  socialImageAlt="An open-plan Australian kitchen, dining and living area heated by a wall-mounted reverse-cycle air conditioner"
  imagePosition="68% center"
>
  <p>An open-plan living area can be much harder to heat than its furniture layout suggests.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/categories/living-spaces/">lounge</a> may occupy one end of the room, but the heater may also be warming the <a href="/categories/kitchen/">kitchen</a>, dining area, hallway, stairwell and every connected space that cannot be closed off.</p>
  <p>High ceilings add more air volume. Large areas of glazing lose heat. Warm air may rise into a void or travel down a hallway before the seating area becomes comfortable.</p>
  <p>This is why a portable heater that works well in a bedroom can seem ineffective in an open-plan room, and why selecting a reverse-cycle system from floor area alone can produce a poor result.</p>
  <p>The useful starting point is the complete connected space: its dimensions, ceiling height, glazing, <a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">insulation</a>, airflow and the way occupants use each zone.</p>
  <p>This guide explains what to check before choosing an <a href="/guides/home-heating-options-australia/">open-plan heating system</a>.</p>
  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>Do not size the heater for the sofa area.</p>
  <p>Measure every area the heating system cannot avoid conditioning.</p>
  <p>A suitable system must deliver enough heat for the total connected volume and distribute it through the occupied zones. It also needs to manage heat loss through windows, external walls, ceilings, doors and uncontrolled gaps.</p>
  <p>Before choosing a larger appliance, consider whether the space can be zoned more effectively and whether curtains, insulation or <a href="/guides/reduce-draughts-before-buying-bigger-heater/">draught reduction</a> would lower the heating requirement.</p>
  <h2>What counts as open plan?</h2>
  <p>An open-plan area may combine:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>kitchen</li>
    <li>dining room</li>
    <li>lounge</li>
    <li>entry</li>
    <li>hallway</li>
    <li><a href="/categories/home-office/">study nook</a></li>
    <li>stairs</li>
    <li>second sitting area</li>
    <li>play area</li>
    <li>indoor–outdoor transition space</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The room does not need to be exceptionally large to create a difficult heating task.</p>
  <p>A moderate living and dining area may become effectively much larger when it opens into:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a long hallway</li>
    <li>an unheated kitchen</li>
    <li>a stairwell</li>
    <li>a double-height void</li>
    <li>another room without a door</li>
    <li>an open laundry or mudroom</li>
    <li>a frequently used external entry</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Any space that cannot be separated is part of the heating load.</p>
  <h2>Heating options at a glance</h2>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Heating options at a glance comparison">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <caption>Heating options at a glance</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Heating option</th><th scope="col">Open-plan suitability</th><th scope="col">Main advantage</th><th scope="col">Main limitation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Single <a href="/guides/reverse-cycle-heating-explained/">reverse-cycle split system</a></td><td>Can suit a moderate connected area when correctly sized and positioned</td><td>Efficient heating and <a href="/guides/air-conditioning-buying-guide/">summer cooling</a></td><td>Air may not reach around corners or into distant zones</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multiple split systems</td><td>Can serve separate parts of a large or irregular layout</td><td>Better control of several occupied zones</td><td>More indoor units, outdoor capacity and installation planning</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multi-split system</td><td>Can connect several indoor units to one outdoor system</td><td>Reduces the number of outdoor units</td><td>Shared capacity and greater design complexity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ducted reverse-cycle heating</td><td>Can condition several connected rooms or zones</td><td>Central distribution and zoning</td><td>Duct losses, installation complexity and minimum-airflow requirements</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ducted gas heating</td><td>Can provide central whole-home heat</td><td>Existing systems may already distribute through the home</td><td>Fuel use, servicing and lack of summer cooling</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wood heater</td><td>Can provide substantial heat in suitable homes</td><td>Strong central radiant and convective warmth</td><td>Installation, emissions, fuel, clearances and uneven distribution</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Portable resistance heater</td><td>Generally poorly suited to large connected areas</td><td>Low purchase complexity</td><td>Limited reach and high electrical demand for the heat delivered</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Electric fireplace</td><td>Usually supplementary in an open-plan room</td><td>Ambience and local heating</td><td>Heating section often performs like another resistance heater</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/guides/ceiling-fans-before-you-buy/">Ceiling fan</a></td><td>Complements another heating source</td><td>Redistributes warm air near high ceilings</td><td>Does not create heat</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>Measure the connected floor area</h2>
  <p>Start with the floor dimensions of every connected zone.</p>
  <p>Measure:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>lounge</li>
    <li>dining area</li>
    <li>kitchen</li>
    <li>open entry</li>
    <li>hallways without doors</li>
    <li>study nook</li>
    <li>stair opening</li>
    <li>connected second living zone</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not stop at visual boundaries created by:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>rugs</li>
    <li>furniture</li>
    <li>kitchen islands</li>
    <li>changes in flooring</li>
    <li>ceiling features</li>
    <li>pendant lighting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These features divide how the room is used, but they do not stop heated air moving through the space.</p>
  <p>Create a simple floor plan and note which openings can genuinely be closed.</p>
  <h2>Include ceiling height</h2>
  <p>Heating demand is affected by the volume of air, not floor area alone.</p>
  <p>A room measuring 50 square metres with a standard ceiling is a different task from the same floor area beneath:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a raked ceiling</li>
    <li>cathedral ceiling</li>
    <li>double-height void</li>
    <li>mezzanine</li>
    <li>open staircase</li>
    <li>raised ceiling over the living zone</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Record the height at:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the lowest point</li>
    <li>the highest point</li>
    <li>the main occupied zone</li>
    <li>stair or void openings</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A simple average height may still miss how warm air behaves in an irregular room.</p>
  <p>High ceilings can allow heat to collect well above the occupants.</p>
  <h2>Warm air does not stay in the lounge zone</h2>
  <p>Warm air rises and moves according to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>temperature differences</li>
    <li>fan operation</li>
    <li>door openings</li>
    <li>stairwells</li>
    <li>ceiling height</li>
    <li>supply-air direction</li>
    <li>return-air position</li>
    <li>exhaust fans</li>
    <li>draughts</li>
    <li>pressure differences</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A heater positioned beside the lounge may warm:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the ceiling</li>
    <li>the kitchen</li>
    <li>the upper stair landing</li>
    <li>an open hallway</li>
  </ul>
  <p>before the far end of the sofa feels comfortable.</p>
  <p>The issue may be system capacity, but it may also be poor distribution.</p>
  <p>Before increasing output, check whether warm air is being directed towards the occupied area or escaping into a connected void.</p>
  <h2>Reverse-cycle split systems</h2>
  <p>A correctly sized wall-mounted reverse-cycle system can be an effective option for a moderate open-plan area.</p>
  <p>It transfers heat from outdoors rather than creating all its heat through electrical resistance, allowing it to deliver substantially more room heat for the electricity used than standard portable resistance heaters.</p>
  <p>Its success depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heating capacity</li>
    <li>local climate</li>
    <li>low-temperature performance</li>
    <li>indoor-unit position</li>
    <li>louvre range</li>
    <li>fan speed</li>
    <li>outdoor-unit position</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>glazing</li>
    <li>connected volume</li>
    <li>room shape</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A high-capacity indoor unit on one wall may still fail to distribute heat around corners or through a long irregular room.</p>
  <p>The unit should face the main occupied area and have an unobstructed path through as much of the connected space as practical.</p>
  <h2>One split system or more than one?</h2>
  <p>One large system may suit a compact rectangular open-plan room where air can move clearly from one end to the other.</p>
  <p>More than one indoor unit may be worth considering where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the layout is long</li>
    <li>the room turns a corner</li>
    <li>one zone is separated by cabinetry</li>
    <li>ceiling heights vary</li>
    <li>the kitchen and lounge have different comfort needs</li>
    <li>one zone is used without the other</li>
    <li>a stairwell draws heat away</li>
    <li>the main unit cannot reach a secondary sitting area</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Two smaller systems are not automatically preferable.</p>
  <p>They introduce:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>additional indoor units</li>
    <li>more wall space</li>
    <li>more controls</li>
    <li>additional maintenance</li>
    <li>more complex outdoor-unit arrangements</li>
    <li>possible simultaneous-load considerations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The choice requires a site-specific assessment.</p>
  <h2>Multi-split systems</h2>
  <p>A multi-split system connects several indoor units to one outdoor system.</p>
  <p>It can suit properties with limited outdoor-unit space or several indoor zones.</p>
  <p>Before choosing one, ask how the proposed combination behaves when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>one indoor unit operates alone</li>
    <li>several units operate together</li>
    <li>all units request maximum heating</li>
    <li>zones require different temperatures</li>
    <li>one unit is switched off</li>
    <li>the outdoor system reaches its combined capacity</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The sum of the indoor units’ nominal capacities may be greater than the outdoor unit can provide simultaneously.</p>
  <p>This can be acceptable in a properly designed system where not every zone reaches peak demand together, but it must be understood before purchase.</p>
  <h2>Ducted reverse-cycle heating</h2>
  <p>A ducted system may suit a larger open-plan area connected to bedrooms or other regularly occupied rooms.</p>
  <p>It uses a central indoor unit and distributes heated air through ducts and grilles.</p>
  <p>Important design considerations include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>total heat load</li>
    <li>duct routes</li>
    <li>duct insulation</li>
    <li>supply-grille positions</li>
    <li>return-air position</li>
    <li>zone layout</li>
    <li>minimum open area</li>
    <li>pressure balance</li>
    <li>roof or underfloor access</li>
    <li>indoor-unit service access</li>
    <li>outdoor-unit placement</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A ducted system can appear visually simple from inside the room, but its performance depends heavily on hidden design and installation.</p>
  <p>Poorly insulated or damaged ducts can lose heat before it reaches the room.</p>
  <h2>Ducted zoning</h2>
  <p>Zoning allows selected parts of a ducted system to operate separately.</p>
  <p>It can reduce unnecessary heating of unoccupied areas, but the system must maintain required airflow.</p>
  <p>Do not assume:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>every zone can close simultaneously</li>
    <li>one small room can operate alone</li>
    <li>more zones are always better</li>
    <li>closing outlets manually is equivalent to designed zoning</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>which zones can operate together</li>
    <li>the minimum area that must remain open</li>
    <li>whether a bypass or variable-airflow system is used</li>
    <li>where the thermostat or sensors are located</li>
    <li>whether the open-plan area is one zone or several</li>
    <li>what happens when only the smallest zone requests heat</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An incorrectly operated or designed zoning system can create noise, poor airflow or equipment problems.</p>
  <h2>Portable heaters</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/fan-heater-vs-ceramic-heater/">Portable fan, ceramic</a>, <a href="/guides/oil-column-heater-vs-panel-heater/">oil column and panel heaters</a> are generally better suited to smaller enclosed rooms.</p>
  <p>In an open-plan area, a portable heater may:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>warm only the person directly beside it</li>
    <li>operate continuously</li>
    <li>fail to raise the temperature of the full space</li>
    <li>encourage several heaters to be used simultaneously</li>
    <li>create cord and clearance problems</li>
    <li>occupy a walkway or furniture zone</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A portable heater may still provide short supervised personal warmth at a desk or chair.</p>
  <p>That is not the same as heating the complete open-plan room.</p>
  <p>Do not use multiple high-powered portable heaters on overloaded power boards or adaptors.</p>
  <p>Current Product Safety guidance recommends one appliance per power point and at least one metre of clearance from combustible materials.</p>
  <h2>Electric fireplaces</h2>
  <p>Electric fireplaces combine a visual flame effect with an electric resistance heater.</p>
  <p>The flame display may create ambience, but the heating section should be assessed like another electric room heater.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heating output</li>
    <li>fan noise</li>
    <li>outlet position</li>
    <li>wall or recess requirements</li>
    <li>electrical supply</li>
    <li>television clearance</li>
    <li>cabinetry ventilation</li>
    <li>servicing access</li>
    <li>whether heat can reach the occupied area</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A visually large electric fireplace may still have a heating output better suited to a smaller enclosed room than an open-plan living area.</p>
  <p>Do not assume the fireplace format increases heating capacity.</p>
  <h2>Wood heating</h2>
  <p>A properly installed wood heater can provide substantial heat in suitable climates and homes.</p>
  <p>Its performance depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heater type</li>
    <li>room layout</li>
    <li>flue design</li>
    <li>fuel quality</li>
    <li>clearances</li>
    <li>air supply</li>
    <li>maintenance</li>
    <li>local regulations</li>
    <li>smoke-management requirements</li>
    <li>distribution to distant zones</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A central wood heater may warm the main living area strongly while bedrooms or spaces around corners remain cool.</p>
  <p>High ceilings can also allow heat to collect above the occupied level.</p>
  <p>Installation requires site-specific professional advice and compliance with applicable requirements.</p>
  <p>An open fireplace is not equivalent to an efficient enclosed wood heater and can draw heated indoor air up the chimney.</p>
  <h2>Gas heating</h2>
  <p>Existing homes may use:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ducted gas heating</li>
    <li>fixed flued gas heaters</li>
    <li>unflued gas heaters</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Gas appliances require correct installation, servicing and ventilation.</p>
  <p>Do not block ventilation openings to retain more heat.</p>
  <p>Unflued gas heating introduces combustion products and moisture into the room and can worsen condensation.</p>
  <p>Never use:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>outdoor gas heaters</li>
    <li>barbecues</li>
    <li>charcoal appliances</li>
    <li>camping heaters intended for outdoor use</li>
  </ul>
  <p>inside the home.</p>
  <p>A household considering a new system should compare reverse-cycle heating rather than assuming the existing fuel type remains the most appropriate option.</p>
  <h2>High ceilings</h2>
  <p>High ceilings increase the heated volume and encourage warm air to collect above the occupied zone.</p>
  <p>Possible responses include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a correctly sized system</li>
    <li>downward louvre direction</li>
    <li>higher indoor fan speed during warm-up</li>
    <li>a reversible ceiling fan</li>
    <li>more effective zoning</li>
    <li>improved roof and ceiling insulation</li>
    <li>reducing uncontrolled leakage into upper spaces</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A larger appliance alone may deliver more heat while much of it remains near the ceiling.</p>
  <h2>Ceiling fans in <a href="/seasonal/">winter</a></h2>
  <p>A reversible ceiling fan can help redistribute warm air that has collected near the ceiling.</p>
  <p>Use:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the manufacturer’s labelled winter setting</li>
    <li>a low suitable speed</li>
    <li>a direction and setting that avoids an uncomfortable direct draught</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not assume winter mode is always clockwise or anticlockwise. The correct direction depends on the fan design and viewing position.</p>
  <p>A ceiling fan:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>does not create heat</li>
    <li>does not replace insulation</li>
    <li>does not increase heater capacity</li>
    <li>does not solve an undersized system</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It can help distribute heat more effectively through a high or open room.</p>
  <h2>Stairwells and upper levels</h2>
  <p>An open stairwell can act as a path for warm air to rise away from the living zone.</p>
  <p>Before sizing a heater, consider whether the stair connects to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>an upper hallway</li>
    <li>bedrooms</li>
    <li>a mezzanine</li>
    <li>a large void</li>
    <li>an unheated level</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Possible options may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>zoning upper and lower levels separately</li>
    <li>adding an appropriate door during renovation</li>
    <li>separate heating systems</li>
    <li>careful indoor-unit placement</li>
    <li>ceiling-fan assistance</li>
    <li>improved insulation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not install temporary curtains or barriers that interfere with safe stair access, handrails, smoke movement or emergency escape.</p>
  <h2>Large windows and glazed doors</h2>
  <p>Open-plan living areas often contain extensive glazing.</p>
  <p>Heat can be lost through:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>single glazing</li>
    <li>metal frames</li>
    <li>sliding doors</li>
    <li>poor seals</li>
    <li>uncovered glass</li>
    <li>large frame perimeters</li>
  </ul>
  <p>People may also feel cold beside glazing even when the air temperature is reasonable.</p>
  <p>Check whether window coverings:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>cover the full glass area</li>
    <li>extend beyond the frame</li>
    <li>close without large side gaps</li>
    <li>reach the sill or floor where intended</li>
    <li>sit close to the wall</li>
    <li>include a pelmet or enclosed upper treatment where suitable</li>
    <li>remain clear of heaters</li>
    <li>can open for useful winter sun</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Window furnishings can improve comfort, but they do not repair damaged seals or substitute for suitable glazing where the problem is substantial.</p>
  <h2>Use useful winter sun</h2>
  <p>Direct winter sun can provide passive warmth in suitable climates and orientations.</p>
  <p>Where appropriate:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>open curtains when sun reaches the room</li>
    <li>allow sunlight to warm internal surfaces</li>
    <li>close coverings before outdoor temperatures fall</li>
    <li>avoid blocking useful sun with furniture or stored items</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Passive heating is more relevant in mixed and cool climate zones than in every part of Australia.</p>
  <p>In warmer northern regions, year-round shading and cooling performance may remain the stronger priority.</p>
  <p>Do not redesign summer shading solely to gain a small amount of winter sun without considering overheating.</p>
  <h2>Insulation</h2>
  <p>Open-plan rooms often have a large ceiling and external-wall area.</p>
  <p>Heat loss may occur through:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ceiling</li>
    <li>roof</li>
    <li>external walls</li>
    <li>suspended floors</li>
    <li>slab edges</li>
    <li>adjoining garages</li>
    <li>raked ceilings</li>
    <li>uninsulated additions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before increasing heater capacity, check whether insulation is:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>present</li>
    <li>dry</li>
    <li>continuous</li>
    <li>disturbed by ducts or electrical work</li>
    <li>compressed</li>
    <li>missing around edges</li>
    <li>suitable for the climate and construction</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not enter a roof space casually.</p>
  <p>Use existing records or arrange an appropriate assessment.</p>
  <p>A larger system can compensate for poor insulation only by operating harder and longer.</p>
  <h2>Draughts and uncontrolled air leakage</h2>
  <p>Warm air can leave through:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>external doors</li>
    <li>sliding-door seals</li>
    <li>windows</li>
    <li>exhaust fans</li>
    <li>ceiling hatches</li>
    <li>floor gaps</li>
    <li>unused fireplaces</li>
    <li>open hallways</li>
    <li>service penetrations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Reduce avoidable leakage without blocking:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>required ventilation</li>
    <li>combustion-air openings</li>
    <li>weep holes</li>
    <li>drainage paths</li>
    <li>subfloor vents</li>
    <li>exhaust systems</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An open-plan room often has several external and internal boundaries, so multiple small leaks can combine into a significant heating problem.</p>
  <h2>Kitchen exhaust and heating</h2>
  <p>The kitchen forms part of many open-plan areas.</p>
  <p>A ducted rangehood removes:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>cooking vapour</li>
    <li>odours</li>
    <li>pollutants</li>
    <li>heated indoor air</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Replacement air then enters the home through available openings.</p>
  <p>This does not mean the rangehood should be avoided.</p>
  <p>Use it when cooking requires extraction, but recognise that:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>high airflow can cool the room</li>
    <li>open doors or windows may alter heating</li>
    <li>a failed backdraft damper may allow leakage when the rangehood is off</li>
    <li>an oversized or incorrectly operated system can affect pressure</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not block or seal the rangehood duct.</p>
  <p>Where a combustion appliance shares the space, qualified advice may be required about ventilation and pressure interactions.</p>
  <h2>Heat produced by cooking</h2>
  <p>Ovens, cooktops and other appliances add heat to an open-plan room.</p>
  <p>This can temporarily reduce heating demand while cooking, but it should not be treated as a heating strategy.</p>
  <p>Never use an oven or cooktop to heat the room.</p>
  <p>Once cooking stops and the rangehood operates, the temperature can change quickly.</p>
  <p>The heating system should be selected for normal room conditions rather than the temporary heat produced during meal preparation.</p>
  <h2>Thermostat and sensor position</h2>
  <p>The thermostat may be located:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>in a wall-mounted indoor unit</li>
    <li>in a remote control</li>
    <li>in a wall controller</li>
    <li>near the return-air grille</li>
    <li>in one ducted zone</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Its reading may not represent every part of an open-plan room.</p>
  <p>For example:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a sensor high on the wall may detect warm air near the ceiling</li>
    <li>a kitchen sensor may be affected by cooking</li>
    <li>direct sun may warm the controller</li>
    <li>a return-air sensor may average several zones</li>
    <li>a sensor beside a doorway may detect colder incoming air</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask where the temperature is measured and whether additional zone sensors are available.</p>
  <p>Do not place a portable thermostat or remote sensor beside:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heaters</li>
    <li>windows</li>
    <li>appliances</li>
    <li>direct sun</li>
    <li>cooking equipment</li>
  </ul>
  <p>unless the manufacturer intends that location.</p>
  <h2>Indoor-unit position</h2>
  <p>For a wall-mounted split system, check whether the unit:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>faces the main occupied area</li>
    <li>can direct air downwards</li>
    <li>can distribute air across the long dimension of the room</li>
    <li>avoids blowing directly onto seating</li>
    <li>remains clear of curtains</li>
    <li>remains clear of cabinetry</li>
    <li>allows filter access</li>
    <li>avoids sending most heat into a hallway</li>
    <li>avoids conflict with ceiling fans or pendant lights</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A unit placed at one end of a long room may heat the nearest zone well while the far end remains cool.</p>
  <h2>Outdoor-unit position</h2>
  <p>The outdoor unit needs:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>clear intake and discharge airflow</li>
    <li>service access</li>
    <li>drainage</li>
    <li>suitable mounting</li>
    <li>acceptable noise impact</li>
    <li>protection from damaging debris without enclosure</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Consider proximity to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>bedrooms</li>
    <li>neighbours</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/outdoor-entertaining-area-setup-what-to-plan-before-buying-extra-furniture-and-accessories/">outdoor entertaining areas</a></li>
    <li>narrow boundaries</li>
    <li>roof runoff</li>
    <li>garden beds</li>
    <li>salt exposure</li>
    <li>paths</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not hide the unit inside a decorative box or dense screen that prevents airflow.</p>
  <h2>Noise</h2>
  <p>Open-plan rooms are used for:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>conversation</li>
    <li>television</li>
    <li>music</li>
    <li>dining</li>
    <li>work</li>
    <li>phone calls</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Heating noise may come from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>indoor fans</li>
    <li>outdoor compressors</li>
    <li>duct airflow</li>
    <li>grilles</li>
    <li>zoning dampers</li>
    <li>thermostat relays</li>
    <li>ceiling fans</li>
    <li>portable heater fans</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check noise at:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>low fan speed</li>
    <li>normal heating output</li>
    <li>maximum warm-up</li>
    <li>defrost operation</li>
    <li>night-time outdoor conditions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A quiet system at minimum output may become noticeably louder during cold-weather warm-up.</p>
  <h2>Heating schedules</h2>
  <p>An open-plan area may not need the same temperature all day.</p>
  <p>Consider:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>when the room is first occupied</li>
    <li>morning use</li>
    <li>evening use</li>
    <li>work-from-home hours</li>
    <li>meal times</li>
    <li>solar generation</li>
    <li>winter sun</li>
    <li>unoccupied periods</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A schedule can pre-warm the room before use and reduce unnecessary operation after occupants leave.</p>
  <p>Do not begin heating many hours early without checking how quickly the room actually warms and retains heat.</p>
  <p>Automatic controls should remain understandable and easy to override.</p>
  <h2>Solar electricity</h2>
  <p>A household with rooftop solar may be able to run reverse-cycle heating during daytime generation.</p>
  <p>The benefit depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>winter solar output</li>
    <li>cloud</li>
    <li>system size</li>
    <li>other household loads</li>
    <li>heating demand</li>
    <li>electricity tariff</li>
    <li>feed-in tariff</li>
    <li>thermal performance of the home</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Solar electricity does not make heating free in every circumstance.</p>
  <p>An open-plan room that loses heat quickly may still require substantial evening grid electricity.</p>
  <p>Insulation, curtains and draught reduction can help retain daytime warmth.</p>
  <h2>Room zoning without permanent walls</h2>
  <p>Furniture and rugs define how an open-plan room is used, but do not stop airflow.</p>
  <p>More effective zoning may involve:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>closing existing internal doors</li>
    <li>using separate heating systems</li>
    <li>controlling ducted zones</li>
    <li>altering the layout during renovation</li>
    <li>adding suitable doors or partitions</li>
    <li>separating stairways where safe and appropriate</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not use temporary barriers that:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>obstruct exits</li>
    <li>interfere with smoke alarms</li>
    <li>block return air</li>
    <li>create trip hazards</li>
    <li>sit near heaters</li>
    <li>interfere with ventilation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A permanent layout change requires consideration of building, ventilation and fire-safety requirements.</p>
  <h2>Children and pets</h2>
  <p>Open-plan rooms are often high-traffic household spaces.</p>
  <p>Portable heaters can create risks through:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>hot surfaces</li>
    <li>cords</li>
    <li>unstable placement</li>
    <li>toys entering clearance zones</li>
    <li>pet bedding</li>
    <li>clothing</li>
    <li>furniture movement</li>
    <li>accidental covering</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Keep portable heaters:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>on a flat, stable surface</li>
    <li>outside walkways</li>
    <li>at least one metre from combustible items</li>
    <li>directly connected to a suitable wall power point</li>
    <li>supervised while operating</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A fixed reverse-cycle or ducted system can reduce floor-level appliance risks, but controls, vents and outdoor equipment still require appropriate care.</p>
  <h2>Smoke alarms and combustion safety</h2>
  <p>Heating decisions should be made alongside household fire safety.</p>
  <p>Maintain smoke alarms according to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>manufacturer instructions</li>
    <li>state or territory requirements</li>
    <li>rental obligations</li>
    <li>local fire-service guidance</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Never use outdoor combustion appliances indoors.</p>
  <p>Existing gas or wood systems require:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>correct installation</li>
    <li>servicing</li>
    <li>clearances</li>
    <li>ventilation</li>
    <li>flue maintenance</li>
    <li>fuel management</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not block ventilation to retain more heat.</p>
  <h2>Regional considerations</h2>
  <h3>Tropical and subtropical regions</h3>
  <p>Heating may be occasional.</p>
  <p>A large permanent system may be selected primarily for cooling, but its heating mode can provide winter comfort during cool mornings.</p>
  <p>Priorities may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>low-load operation</li>
    <li>effective cooling and humidity control</li>
    <li>ceiling fans</li>
    <li>room zoning</li>
    <li>avoiding unnecessary heating of the whole connected area</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Temperate coastal regions</h3>
  <p>Reverse-cycle heating may suit regular winter and summer use.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>moderate heating demand</li>
    <li>coastal corrosion</li>
    <li>glazing</li>
    <li>outdoor-unit noise</li>
    <li>connected hallways</li>
    <li>open-plan air distribution</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Hot-arid and inland regions</h3>
  <p>Warm days can be followed by cold nights.</p>
  <p>Thermal mass, curtains, daytime sun and controlled evening heating may be useful.</p>
  <p>Large temperature changes make heat retention important.</p>
  <h3>Cool-temperate and alpine regions</h3>
  <p>Heating capacity and low-temperature performance become more important.</p>
  <p>Open-plan rooms with high ceilings and glazing may require:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>site-specific sizing</li>
    <li>strong insulation</li>
    <li>cold-zone reverse-cycle performance</li>
    <li>effective zoning</li>
    <li>ceiling-fan assistance</li>
    <li>supplementary systems where professionally assessed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not select from a warm-climate room-size chart.</p>
  <h2>Renters</h2>
  <p>Renters may be limited to existing fixed systems or portable appliances.</p>
  <p>Useful steps include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>using supplied heating correctly</li>
    <li>closing unused rooms</li>
    <li>using existing curtains</li>
    <li>removable draught controls</li>
    <li>reporting damaged window seals</li>
    <li>reporting faulty supplied heating</li>
    <li>asking for written approval for fixed installation</li>
    <li>choosing a safe portable-heater location</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not add:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>fixed wiring</li>
    <li>wall-mounted heaters</li>
    <li>split systems</li>
    <li>permanent partitions</li>
    <li>new ducting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>without required approval and licensed work.</p>
  <h2>Apartments and strata properties</h2>
  <p>Open-plan apartment heating may be affected by:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>limited outdoor-unit locations</li>
    <li>balcony airflow</li>
    <li>neighbours</li>
    <li>common walls</li>
    <li>glazing</li>
    <li>body corporate requirements</li>
    <li>facade rules</li>
    <li>condensate drainage</li>
    <li>electrical capacity</li>
    <li>fire-rated construction</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A large wall-mounted unit may fit inside but be impossible to support with an approved outdoor-unit position.</p>
  <p>Confirm all approvals and installation constraints before ordering.</p>
  <h2>What to ask an installer</h2>
  <p>Before accepting a proposal, ask:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>What total area and volume were included?</li>
    <li>Were hallways and stairs included?</li>
    <li>What ceiling heights were used?</li>
    <li>How were windows and glazing considered?</li>
    <li>What insulation assumptions were made?</li>
    <li>What local winter conditions were used?</li>
    <li>What heating capacity is required?</li>
    <li>How does capacity change at lower outdoor temperatures?</li>
    <li>Is one indoor unit sufficient?</li>
    <li>Would multiple units distribute heat better?</li>
    <li>How will the kitchen and lounge be balanced?</li>
    <li>Where is the thermostat or sensor?</li>
    <li>Where will return air be located?</li>
    <li>What duct losses are expected?</li>
    <li>What zones can operate independently?</li>
    <li>What minimum area must remain open?</li>
    <li>How will warm air near high ceilings be redistributed?</li>
    <li>Where will the outdoor unit be placed?</li>
    <li>How will drainage be handled?</li>
    <li>What electrical work is included?</li>
    <li>Who performs the licensed refrigerant work?</li>
    <li>What documentation will be supplied?</li>
    <li>How will filters and equipment be serviced?</li>
    <li>What is excluded from the quote?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not accept a system size based only on a quick floor-area estimate where the room is large, high, irregular or heavily glazed.</p>
  <h2>Before You Buy Checklist</h2>
  <p>Before choosing heating for an open-plan living area, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list" aria-label="Before you buy checklist">
    <li>Which zones are physically connected?</li>
    <li>Which openings can actually be closed?</li>
    <li>What is the total connected floor area?</li>
    <li>What is the ceiling height in each zone?</li>
    <li>Is there a raked, cathedral or double-height ceiling?</li>
    <li>Is there an open stairwell?</li>
    <li>Does the room turn a corner?</li>
    <li>Are hallways included?</li>
    <li>How much external wall area is present?</li>
    <li>How much glazing is present?</li>
    <li>Which direction do the windows face?</li>
    <li>Do curtains and blinds close effectively?</li>
    <li>Can useful winter sun enter?</li>
    <li>What insulation is present?</li>
    <li>Are draughts present?</li>
    <li>Does heat escape into unused rooms?</li>
    <li>Where do occupants sit, eat and work?</li>
    <li>Are all zones occupied at the same time?</li>
    <li>Is summer cooling also required?</li>
    <li>Does the local climate require strong winter performance?</li>
    <li>Has the heating side of the Zoned Energy Rating Label been checked?</li>
    <li>Has heating capacity been separated from electrical input?</li>
    <li>Was the system sized for volume rather than lounge floor area alone?</li>
    <li>Is one split system likely to reach every occupied zone?</li>
    <li>Would multiple indoor units improve distribution?</li>
    <li>Has multi-split shared capacity been explained?</li>
    <li>Is ducted heating practical?</li>
    <li>Are duct routes and insulation suitable?</li>
    <li>Where is the return-air grille?</li>
    <li>Which ducted zones can operate together?</li>
    <li>What minimum area must remain open?</li>
    <li>Is a portable heater being expected to heat too much space?</li>
    <li>Can portable-heater clearances be maintained?</li>
    <li>Can each portable heater use one suitable wall power point?</li>
    <li>Is a wood or gas system being professionally assessed?</li>
    <li>Is a reversible ceiling fan available?</li>
    <li>Can warm air be moved down from high ceilings?</li>
    <li>Will the indoor unit face the occupied zone?</li>
    <li>Can louvres direct air downwards?</li>
    <li>Will furniture block the airflow?</li>
    <li>Will warm air disappear into a hallway or staircase?</li>
    <li>Is the thermostat position representative?</li>
    <li>Will kitchen heat or rangehood use affect temperature control?</li>
    <li>Is outdoor-unit noise acceptable?</li>
    <li>Is the outdoor unit clear of entertaining and bedroom areas?</li>
    <li>Is condensate and defrost drainage defined?</li>
    <li>Has electrical capacity been checked?</li>
    <li>Is switchboard work included?</li>
    <li>Is licensed refrigerant work clearly allocated?</li>
    <li>Are landlord or strata approvals required?</li>
    <li>Can insulation, curtains or draught reduction lower the required capacity?</li>
    <li>Has the written quote defined the complete scope?</li>
    <li>Are product and workmanship support arrangements clear?</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Heat the whole connected space — or reduce what is connected</h2>
  <p>An open-plan living area should not be treated as a small lounge merely because that is where the household sits.</p>
  <p>The heater may also be conditioning the kitchen, dining area, hallway, stairs and the unused air beneath a high ceiling.</p>
  <p>Measure the full connected floor area and volume. Check glazing, insulation, draughts, ceiling height and airflow before comparing systems.</p>
  <p>A correctly sized reverse-cycle or ducted system can provide efficient heating where its air reaches the occupied zones. Ceiling fans can help redistribute warm air, while curtains, insulation and practical zoning can reduce the load.</p>
  <p>Where one appliance is being asked to heat several poorly separated spaces, the better answer may be improved zoning or multiple appropriately designed systems—not simply buying the largest unit that fits on the wall.</p>

